{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019gZnWX7QMGGjqD75JL4k3C","type":"tool_result","content":"979\t**Q4. Answer: B.** `status` describes the current state of the object, supplied and updated by the Kubernetes system and its components [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n980\t- **A** inverts the authorship boundary.\n981\t- **C** is wrong: `status` is part of the stored object, not a display-time computation by the client.\n982\t- **D** is wrong for the same reason. It is a real, persisted field on the object.\n983\t\n984\t**Q5. Answer: B.** `apiVersion` is which version of the Kubernetes API you're using to create this object [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23], and it is one of the fields set per object.\n985\t- **A** conflates the API version of a resource type with the version of the cluster software. Those move independently; a resource's API version can remain stable across many cluster releases.\n986\t- **C** is the file-level assumption, and it fails because a single file can hold several objects of different kinds, each versioned independently.\n987\t- **D** is wrong: the four required fields are required for every object, built-in or custom.\n988\t\n989\t**Q6. Answer: C.** Low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n990\t- **A** and **D** are among the documentation's own examples of namespaced objects.\n991\t- **B** is namespaced too, and pointedly so. ServiceAccounts are bound to a namespace, and every namespace gets a `default` one on creation [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23].\n992\t\n993\t**Q7. Answer: B.** It is not necessary to use multiple namespaces to separate slightly different resources, such as different versions of the same software; use labels to distinguish resources within the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n994\t- **A** is the intuitive habit the documentation specifically corrects.\n995\t- **C** additionally fails on nesting: namespaces cannot be nested inside one another [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n996\t- **D** misuses ResourceQuota, which divides cluster resources between namespaces rather than linking them.\n997\t\n998\t**Q8. Answer: B.** `kube-node-lease` holds Lease objects associated with each node; node leases allow the kubelet to send heartbeats so that the control plane can detect node failure [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n999\t- **A** invents an authorization mechanism; leases here are liveness signals, not credentials.\n1000\t- **C** describes binding, which is the scheduler notifying the API server of its placement decision, a different mechanism entirely.\n1001\t- **D** is the definition of `kube-system`, and picking it means the four initial namespaces have blurred together. They are worth separating.\n1002\t\n1003\t**Q9. Answer: C.** Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another, and each Kubernetes resource can only be in one namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n1004\t- **A** and **B** contradict those two rules directly.\n1005\t- **D** is the subtle one and it is still wrong: cluster-scoped resources do not belong to every namespace. They belong to none. That distinction matters enormously for permissions, because \"in all namespaces\" and \"in no namespace\" imply very different things about who can be granted access *[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 §3]*.\n1006\t\n1007\t**Q10. Answer: B.** Kubernetes Secrets are, by default, stored unencrypted in the API server's underlying data store; anyone with API access can retrieve or modify one, as can anyone with etcd access or the ability to create a Pod in the namespace [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23]. What the object type gives you is a place to apply the four recommended protections, not the protections themselves.\n1008\t- **A** is the misconception this chapter exists to correct.\n1009\t- **C** is wrong on the premise: API traffic is TLS-protected as a matter of cluster configuration [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23], and that protection is not specific to Secrets.\n1010\t- **D** contradicts the documented behavior outright.\n1011\t\n1012\t**Q11. Answer: C.** For the environment-variable path, as for command-and-args and read-only volume file paths, the kubelet uses the data from the ConfigMap when it launches the containers for a Pod [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Only the API-reading path subscribes to updates.\n1013\t- **A** and **B** both assume a propagation mechanism that does not exist for this path.\n1014\t- **D** imagines a consistency check the platform does not perform; the container simply holds the value it was launched with.\n1015\t\n1016\t**Q12. Answer: C.** Once a ConfigMap is marked as immutable, it is not possible to revert this change nor to mutate the contents of the `data` or `binaryData` fields; you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Note the parallel to Chapter 2's image rule: the response to \"this needs to change\" is *make a new one*, not *edit this one* [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n1017\t- **A** invents a privilege distinction. Immutability is a property of the object, not of the caller.\n1018\t- **B** is the most tempting wrong answer because it sounds like a reasonable safety valve. There is no such escape hatch.\n1019\t- **D** is wrong: both fields are covered.\n1020\t\n1021\t**Q13. Answer: D.** The data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed 1 MiB; for larger settings, consider mounting a volume or using a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n1022\t- **A** is a workaround for a limit that exists precisely because ConfigMaps are not designed to hold large chunks of data; it fights the design rather than following the guidance.\n1023\t- **B** is wrong on its premise: a Secret is described as holding *a small amount* of sensitive data [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23], and is not the escape hatch for size.\n1024\t- **C** misreads the limit as advisory.\n1025\t\n1026\t**Q14. Answer: C.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. The configuration has to exist in `team-a`.\n1027\t- **A** invents a cross-namespace reference syntax that the field does not accept.\n1028\t- **B** borrows the Service DNS form, which addresses Services over the network [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. A ConfigMap is not reached over the network by a Pod's config machinery, and these are two mechanisms that look similar and are not.\n1029\t- **D** is the closest to plausible and still wrong: permission to read an object is not the same as the ability to reference it as a config source. Access control governs *who may*, not *what the field accepts*.\n1030\t\n1031\t**Q15. Answer: B.** Set-based selectors use `in`, `notin`, and `exists` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n1032\t- **A**, **C**, and **D** are all equality-based: `=`, `==`, and `!=` are the equality-based operators. `!=` in particular reads like a set operation and is not one.\n1033\t\n1034\t**Q16. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n1035\t- **A** uses an operator that does not exist in this vocabulary; the operators are `In`, `NotIn`, `Exists`, and `DoesNotExist`.\n1036\t- **C** is a real operator with a different meaning: `Exists` tests for the key regardless of value, so it would also match `app: api`.\n1037\t- **D** is `In` with an empty value list, which matches nothing.\n1038\t\n1039\t**Q17. Answer: B.** Non-identifying information should be recorded using annotations [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]; selection is a label operation, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. The fix is not to work around the selector. It is to record the attribute as a label, because wanting to select on it is what makes it identifying.\n1040\t- **A** implies a performance difference where there is a capability difference.\n1041\t- **C** inverts the prefix rule: `kubernetes.io/` and `k8s.io/` are reserved for core components, not required of user keys.\n1042\t- **D** describes namespace scoping, which is real but is not what stops the controller here.\n1043\t\n1044\t**Q18. Answer: B.** Selection operates within the namespace scope of the query; a namespaced query does not cross the boundary [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n1045\t- **A** contradicts the documented behavior: labels can be attached at creation time and subsequently added and modified at any time, with no difference in how they select [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n1046\t- **C** invents an interaction between annotations and labels. They are independent maps.\n1047\t- **D** invents a result limit.\n1048\t\n1049\t**Q19. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a selector, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive; it answers *which objects* [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. `replicas` sits in `spec` and states desired state: how many should exist, which is exactly the documentation's own worked example of a Deployment spec specifying three replicas [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n1050\t- **A** inverts the two.\n1051\t- **C** and **D** both misplace authorship: `replicas` is something you set in `spec`, not something reported to you in `status`. `status` reports how many *currently* exist, which is the other half of the pair and the thing the controller compares against.\n1052\t\n1053\t---\n1054\t\n1055\t## Chapter Summary\n1056\t\n1057\t| Concept | Remember This |\n1058\t|---|---|\n1059\t| Kubernetes object | A persistent entity, a \"record of intent.\" Create it and the system constantly works to ensure it exists [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23] |\n1060\t| The four fields | `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, `spec`. Every object. Every time. Which API, which kind, which one, what it should look like |\n1061\t| `spec` | What you want. You write it |\n1062\t| `status` | What is. The system writes it. Every controller exists to close the distance between the two |\n1063\t| Manifest | The file you write the object in. YAML by convention. Submit with `kubectl apply -f` |\n1064\t| Namespace | A scope for names. Unique within, not across. Cannot be nested. One namespace per resource |\n1065\t| Initial namespaces | `default` (start without creating one), `kube-system` (system objects), `kube-public` (readable by all, by convention), `kube-node-lease` (node heartbeat Leases) |\n1066\t| Cluster-scoped | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespace objects themselves. `kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false` settles it |\n1067\t| ConfigMap | Non-confidential key-value data, ≤1 MiB, same namespace as the Pod. Four consumption paths; only the API-reading one sees updates. Immutability is irreversible |\n1068\t| Secret | Same shape, different intent and treatment. Stored unencrypted by default. Readable by anyone with API access, etcd access, or Pod-creation rights in the namespace |\n1069\t| `dockerconfigjson` | The Secret type behind `imagePullSecrets`, a serialized `~/.docker/config.json` |\n1070\t| Label | Key/value identifying attribute. Selectable. Means nothing to the core system, which is why it works everywhere |\n1071\t| Label selector | **The core grouping primitive in Kubernetes.** Equality-based (`=`, `==`, `!=`) and set-based (`in`, `notin`, `exists`), ANDed with commas |\n1072\t| `matchLabels` | Exactly equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` |\n1073\t| Annotation | Non-identifying metadata. Recordable, not selectable. If you might search on it, it's a label |\n1074\t\n1075\t---\n1076\t\n1077\t## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n1078\t\n1079\t**Voyage Progress:** 🗺️ → 🌊 → 🌅 — Chapter 4 of 15 complete. You have crossed from *watching the system* to *writing to it*, which is the largest single step in this book.\n1080\t\n1081\tYou can now read a manifest you have never seen. You know which of its fields you author and which one is a report. You know where its name has to be unique and which resources have no name-scope at all. You know how to describe a set of objects rather than list one. And you know the exact thing a Secret does and does not do, which is a piece of knowledge a surprising number of working practitioners have wrong.\n1082\t\n1083\t---\n1084\t\n1085\t## The Voyage Ahead\n1086\t\n1087\tYou have written declarations for five kinds of thing. You have not yet written one for the thing that actually runs.\n1088\t\n1089\tChapter 5 is about the Pod: the object that represents a set of one or more running containers on your cluster, and the unit that everything else in Kubernetes ultimately arranges [source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23]. Everything you learned here applies to it unchanged: four fields, a `spec` you write, a `status` you read. But a Pod's `status` has something the objects in this chapter did not: a **phase**, a small vocabulary of words describing where in its short life the Pod currently is. And that vocabulary is the first thing you look at when something is wrong.\n1090\t\n1091\tThere is a second thing to watch for. A Pod is described in the documentation as *relatively ephemeral rather than durable*, and it is never rescheduled to a different node. If the node it is on dies, it is not moved; it is replaced by a new, near-identical Pod with a different UID [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. Hold that against what you learned in §1. If the thing that runs your application is designed to be replaced rather than repaired, then a declaration that names a specific Pod is a declaration about something disposable, and something else must be holding the intent that survives it.\n1092\t\n1093\tChapter 5 introduces the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent.\n1094\t\n1095\t> *\"You have stopped issuing orders. Everything after this is learning what reads them.\"*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a71be12-5e8e-49d8-8169-5c316e76d768","uuid":"fb0e27a7-506b-43df-874a-dc520196b8c8","timestamp":"2026-08-24T09:05:39.333Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-04\\draft-v1.md","content":"**Q4. Answer: B.** `status` describes the current state of the object, supplied and updated by the Kubernetes system and its components [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n- **A** inverts the authorship boundary.\n- **C** is wrong: `status` is part of the stored object, not a display-time computation by the client.\n- **D** is wrong for the same reason. It is a real, persisted field on the object.\n\n**Q5. Answer: B.** `apiVersion` is which version of the Kubernetes API you're using to create this object [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23], and it is one of the fields set per object.\n- **A** conflates the API version of a resource type with the version of the cluster software. Those move independently; a resource's API version can remain stable across many cluster releases.\n- **C** is the file-level assumption, and it fails because a single file can hold several objects of different kinds, each versioned independently.\n- **D** is wrong: the four required fields are required for every object, built-in or custom.\n\n**Q6. Answer: C.** Low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** and **D** are among the documentation's own examples of namespaced objects.\n- **B** is namespaced too, and pointedly so. ServiceAccounts are bound to a namespace, and every namespace gets a `default` one on creation [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23].\n\n**Q7. Answer: B.** It is not necessary to use multiple namespaces to separate slightly different resources, such as different versions of the same software; use labels to distinguish resources within the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** is the intuitive habit the documentation specifically corrects.\n- **C** additionally fails on nesting: namespaces cannot be nested inside one another [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **D** misuses ResourceQuota, which divides cluster resources between namespaces rather than linking them.\n\n**Q8. Answer: B.** `kube-node-lease` holds Lease objects associated with each node; node leases allow the kubelet to send heartbeats so that the control plane can detect node failure [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** invents an authorization mechanism; leases here are liveness signals, not credentials.\n- **C** describes binding, which is the scheduler notifying the API server of its placement decision, a different mechanism entirely.\n- **D** is the definition of `kube-system`, and picking it means the four initial namespaces have blurred together. They are worth separating.\n\n**Q9. Answer: C.** Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another, and each Kubernetes resource can only be in one namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** and **B** contradict those two rules directly.\n- **D** is the subtle one and it is still wrong: cluster-scoped resources do not belong to every namespace. They belong to none. That distinction matters enormously for permissions, because \"in all namespaces\" and \"in no namespace\" imply very different things about who can be granted access *[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 §3]*.\n\n**Q10. Answer: B.** Kubernetes Secrets are, by default, stored unencrypted in the API server's underlying data store; anyone with API access can retrieve or modify one, as can anyone with etcd access or the ability to create a Pod in the namespace [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23]. What the object type gives you is a place to apply the four recommended protections, not the protections themselves.\n- **A** is the misconception this chapter exists to correct.\n- **C** is wrong on the premise: API traffic is TLS-protected as a matter of cluster configuration [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23], and that protection is not specific to Secrets.\n- **D** contradicts the documented behavior outright.\n\n**Q11. Answer: C.** For the environment-variable path, as for command-and-args and read-only volume file paths, the kubelet uses the data from the ConfigMap when it launches the containers for a Pod [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Only the API-reading path subscribes to updates.\n- **A** and **B** both assume a propagation mechanism that does not exist for this path.\n- **D** imagines a consistency check the platform does not perform; the container simply holds the value it was launched with.\n\n**Q12. Answer: C.** Once a ConfigMap is marked as immutable, it is not possible to revert this change nor to mutate the contents of the `data` or `binaryData` fields; you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Note the parallel to Chapter 2's image rule: the response to \"this needs to change\" is *make a new one*, not *edit this one* [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n- **A** invents a privilege distinction. Immutability is a property of the object, not of the caller.\n- **B** is the most tempting wrong answer because it sounds like a reasonable safety valve. There is no such escape hatch.\n- **D** is wrong: both fields are covered.\n\n**Q13. Answer: D.** The data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed 1 MiB; for larger settings, consider mounting a volume or using a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n- **A** is a workaround for a limit that exists precisely because ConfigMaps are not designed to hold large chunks of data; it fights the design rather than following the guidance.\n- **B** is wrong on its premise: a Secret is described as holding *a small amount* of sensitive data [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23], and is not the escape hatch for size.\n- **C** misreads the limit as advisory.\n\n**Q14. Answer: C.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. The configuration has to exist in `team-a`.\n- **A** invents a cross-namespace reference syntax that the field does not accept.\n- **B** borrows the Service DNS form, which addresses Services over the network [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. A ConfigMap is not reached over the network by a Pod's config machinery, and these are two mechanisms that look similar and are not.\n- **D** is the closest to plausible and still wrong: permission to read an object is not the same as the ability to reference it as a config source. Access control governs *who may*, not *what the field accepts*.\n\n**Q15. Answer: B.** Set-based selectors use `in`, `notin`, and `exists` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n- **A**, **C**, and **D** are all equality-based: `=`, `==`, and `!=` are the equality-based operators. `!=` in particular reads like a set operation and is not one.\n\n**Q16. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n- **A** uses an operator that does not exist in this vocabulary; the operators are `In`, `NotIn`, `Exists`, and `DoesNotExist`.\n- **C** is a real operator with a different meaning: `Exists` tests for the key regardless of value, so it would also match `app: api`.\n- **D** is `In` with an empty value list, which matches nothing.\n\n**Q17. Answer: B.** Non-identifying information should be recorded using annotations [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]; selection is a label operation, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. The fix is not to work around the selector. It is to record the attribute as a label, because wanting to select on it is what makes it identifying.\n- **A** implies a performance difference where there is a capability difference.\n- **C** inverts the prefix rule: `kubernetes.io/` and `k8s.io/` are reserved for core components, not required of user keys.\n- **D** describes namespace scoping, which is real but is not what stops the controller here.\n\n**Q18. Answer: B.** Selection operates within the namespace scope of the query; a namespaced query does not cross the boundary [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** contradicts the documented behavior: labels can be attached at creation time and subsequently added and modified at any time, with no difference in how they select [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n- **C** invents an interaction between annotations and labels. They are independent maps.\n- **D** invents a result limit.\n\n**Q19. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a selector, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive; it answers *which objects* [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. `replicas` sits in `spec` and states desired state: how many should exist, which is exactly the documentation's own worked example of a Deployment spec specifying three replicas [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n- **A** inverts the two.\n- **C** and **D** both misplace authorship: `replicas` is something you set in `spec`, not something reported to you in `status`. `status` reports how many *currently* exist, which is the other half of the pair and the thing the controller compares against.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n| Concept | Remember This |\n|---|---|\n| Kubernetes object | A persistent entity, a \"record of intent.\" Create it and the system constantly works to ensure it exists [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23] |\n| The four fields | `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, `spec`. Every object. Every time. Which API, which kind, which one, what it should look like |\n| `spec` | What you want. You write it |\n| `status` | What is. The system writes it. Every controller exists to close the distance between the two |\n| Manifest | The file you write the object in. YAML by convention. Submit with `kubectl apply -f` |\n| Namespace | A scope for names. Unique within, not across. Cannot be nested. One namespace per resource |\n| Initial namespaces | `default` (start without creating one), `kube-system` (system objects), `kube-public` (readable by all, by convention), `kube-node-lease` (node heartbeat Leases) |\n| Cluster-scoped | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespace objects themselves. `kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false` settles it |\n| ConfigMap | Non-confidential key-value data, ≤1 MiB, same namespace as the Pod. Four consumption paths; only the API-reading one sees updates. Immutability is irreversible |\n| Secret | Same shape, different intent and treatment. Stored unencrypted by default. Readable by anyone with API access, etcd access, or Pod-creation rights in the namespace |\n| `dockerconfigjson` | The Secret type behind `imagePullSecrets`, a serialized `~/.docker/config.json` |\n| Label | Key/value identifying attribute. Selectable. Means nothing to the core system, which is why it works everywhere |\n| Label selector | **The core grouping primitive in Kubernetes.** Equality-based (`=`, `==`, `!=`) and set-based (`in`, `notin`, `exists`), ANDed with commas |\n| `matchLabels` | Exactly equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` |\n| Annotation | Non-identifying metadata. Recordable, not selectable. If you might search on it, it's a label |\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n\n**Voyage Progress:** 🗺️ → 🌊 → 🌅 — Chapter 4 of 15 complete. You have crossed from *watching the system* to *writing to it*, which is the largest single step in this book.\n\nYou can now read a manifest you have never seen. You know which of its fields you author and which one is a report. You know where its name has to be unique and which resources have no name-scope at all. You know how to describe a set of objects rather than list one. And you know the exact thing a Secret does and does not do, which is a piece of knowledge a surprising number of working practitioners have wrong.\n\n---\n\n## The Voyage Ahead\n\nYou have written declarations for five kinds of thing. You have not yet written one for the thing that actually runs.\n\nChapter 5 is about the Pod: the object that represents a set of one or more running containers on your cluster, and the unit that everything else in Kubernetes ultimately arranges [source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23]. Everything you learned here applies to it unchanged: four fields, a `spec` you write, a `status` you read. But a Pod's `status` has something the objects in this chapter did not: a **phase**, a small vocabulary of words describing where in its short life the Pod currently is. And that vocabulary is the first thing you look at when something is wrong.\n\nThere is a second thing to watch for. A Pod is described in the documentation as *relatively ephemeral rather than durable*, and it is never rescheduled to a different node. If the node it is on dies, it is not moved; it is replaced by a new, near-identical Pod with a different UID [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. Hold that against what you learned in §1. If the thing that runs your application is designed to be replaced rather than repaired, then a declaration that names a specific Pod is a declaration about something disposable, and something else must be holding the intent that survives it.\n\nChapter 5 introduces the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent.\n\n> *\"You have stopped issuing orders. Everything after this is learning what reads them.\"*","numLines":117,"startLine":979,"totalLines":1095}}}
